rules_version = '2';

// Britannia Reports — Firestore security rules
//
// Posture: deny everything, for everyone, authenticated or not.
// F-01 provisions the store but ships zero collections, so there is nothing
// legitimate to allow yet. Every collection is opened by the slice that
// introduces it — never pre-emptively.

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }

  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ILLUSTRATIVE ONLY — NOT LIVE RULE CODE. DO NOT UNCOMMENT AS-IS.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The shape below sketches the per-collection ownership pattern later slices
// will need. It is a sketch, not a template: the collection name and the owner
// field name are both S-01's to choose, and `ownerField` below is a deliberate
// placeholder that will not match anything real.
//
// Placement: this block belongs INSIDE `match /databases/{database}/documents`,
// alongside the deny-all rule above — not here, where it sits outside the
// `service` block and cannot compile.
//
//   match /<collection>/{docId} {
//     allow read, write: if request.auth != null
//                        && request.auth.uid == resource.data.<ownerField>;
//   }
//
// ⚠ READ THIS BEFORE UNCOMMENTING ANYTHING
//
// A single field-name typo in exactly this comparison is the most expensive
// failure mode this project has modelled. The pre-mortem in
// `context/foundation/infrastructure.md` describes it concretely: a rule
// written against `teacherUid` while the documents actually carried
// `teacherId`. The comparison silently evaluated against a missing field,
// the rule silently over-permitted, and one teacher could read another
// teacher's data for a month before anyone noticed. Over-permissive rules do
// not fail loudly — that is the whole problem.
//
// Two consequences, both binding:
//
//   1. The owner field name here must be copied from the actual document
//      schema, not from this comment and not from memory.
//
//   2. Whoever turns this sketch into a live rule writes rules tests FIRST.
//      That means adopting `@firebase/rules-unit-testing` plus the Firebase
//      emulator suite — both deliberately deferred out of F-01, and both
//      recorded as a hard S-01 prerequisite in the plan's Open Risks, not as
//      a nice-to-have. The emulator carries its own runtime prerequisite;
//      see that plan section for the current setup cost.
//
// Minimum coverage before any conditional rule is considered done:
// teacher A can read and write only their own documents; teacher B is denied
// access to teacher A's documents; an unauthenticated caller is denied
// everything.
